This is a new version of XView3.2p1-X11R6 for Linux/ELF. It fixes some bugs
that existed in previous versions. They could cause coredumps or other
miscellaneous failures like ignoring the resoure-database and displaying
PANEL_LISTS with wrong colors.

This release is based on Oleg Kibirev's release of XView for Linux/ELF. It
contains patches from Oleg Kibirev <oleg@gd.cs.CSUFresno.EDU> (his patches
are based mostly on Kenneth Osterberg's patches <lmfken@lmf.ericsson.se> or
<lmfken@bluese1.ericsson.fi>), Wolfram Gloger <u7y22ab@sun4.lrz-muenchen.de>
and myself. If you have other patches for XView-bugs, please send them to
me.

You can find the following files on sunsite.unc.edu in
/pub/Linux/libs/X/xview/elf:

xview3.2p1-X11R6-LinuxELF.1.bin.tar.gz:
  This is the new binary release that contains everything that's required to
  use the XView-libraries.
xview3.2p1-X11R6-LinuxELF.1.clients.tar.gz
  Sourcecode and binaries for some more XView-clients.
xview3.2p1-X11R6-LinuxELF.1.src.tar.gz
  This is the sourcecode for the XView-library (and some clients) with my
  patches (see below) applied.
xview3.2p1-X11R6-LinuxELF.1.diff.gz
  These are my patches relative to the XView3.2p1-X11R6-release as found on
  ftp.x.org
xview3.2p1-X11R6-LinuxELF.1.README
  The file you're reading at the moment
xview3.2p1-X11R6-LinuxELF.1.lsm
  ...and the LSM-entry

To install the binary version, you have 2 options:
If you already installed the previous XView-release for Linux/ELF, it's
sufficient to replace just the files /usr/openwin/lib/lib* with the ones
from the new package and do a "ldconfig /usr/openwin/lib" afterwards. If you
didn't install the old ELF-version of XView, you might want to move your old
/usr/openwin-tree to a save place first. Then cd to / and do a
tar xvpzf /some/path/xview3.2p1-X11R6-LinuxELF.1.bin.tar.gz (as root). After
doing "ldconfig /usr/openwin/lib" (and possibly adding /usr/openwin/lib to
/etc/ld.so.config), you should be able to use the new libraries.

BTW, I chose version 3.2.1 for the shared libraries. This means that they're
compatible with the old versions---you don't have to recompile your apps to
use the new version (if they were liked against the shared libs, that is).

HINT TO DISTRIBUTION-MAINTAINERS:
*PLEASE* replace your current XView-packages with this new one. You can save
your users and all programmers of XView-apps a lot of trouble.

