From: adam@yggdrasil.com (Adam J. Richter)
Subject: comboot 1.0: free floppy .COM file booter (e.g., for network booting)
Date: 1996/04/27
Message-ID: <cola-liw-830600128-3633-1@oravannahka.helsinki.fi>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 151720634
x-original-date: Thu, 25 Apr 96 17:12 PDT
followup-to: comp.os.linux.networking
content-type: text/plain; charset=ISO-8859-1
summary: Primarily for network boot floppies
organization: Yggdrasil Computing, Incorporated
keywords: floppy diskette .COM boot sector
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.os.linux.announce


-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----


	I have transformed the floppy boot code in the 1.3.95 Linux
kernel into a program that will load and run a DOS-style .COM image.
You can use this to make a boot floppy from any DOS-style .COM file,
provided that that file does not make any DOS system calls.

	The primary use for these 512 bytes of software will probably
be to produce freely redistributable network boot disks that work on
any kind of disk drive.  The current etherboot-1.1 package generates
ROM images and .COM files, but in order to make a network boot
diskette, you still need DOS on the diskette to run the .COM file,
and an image of such a disk is only valid for one type of floppy media.

	With comboot, it is possible to make a floppy image that
is freely redistributable and can be written to any type of floppy.
Because the image does not include a Linux kernel and can load all
sorts of other arbitrary kernel arguments via the "bootp" protocol.

	This makes it much easier to deploy diskless (or otherwise
network booted) Linux workstations in institutional settings where X
terminals to other diskless workstations might be used.

	Other uses for comboot would include booting other DOS-based
software on systems on which DOS is not installed.  Such software
cannot make any DOS system calls, but can call the BIOS, so the uses
that this software enables will probably be limited to standalone
kernel-like applications that turn the PC into a dedicated box, such
as a router, non-linux X terminal, industrial controller, or the like.

Begin3
Title:          comboot
Version:        1.0
Entered-date:   25APR96
Description:    A boot sector for booting DOS-style .COM files.
		Used primarily for making freely redistributable
		network boot floppies under Linux (i.e., ones that
		do not require a copy of DOS on the floppy).
Keywords:       floppy diskette boot sector
Author:         adam@yggdrasil.com (Adam J. Richter)
		Comboot is almost entirely derived from code written
	        by Linus Torvalds (torvalds@helsinki.fi),
