-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----


ANNOUNCEMENT

	Using the etherboot-1.1 software package in combination with
the comboot-1.0 boot sector that I have just released, I have produced
freely redistributable floppy images that should work for all types of
floppy disks for booting via ethernet.

	Etherboot was written by Markus Gutschke
(gutschk@math.uni-muenster.de) and Ken Yap (ken@syd.dit.csiro.au).
Comboot is derived from the Linux boot sector written by Linus Torvalds,
Doug Evans and Drew Eckhardt,

	net-boot-floppies-1.1.tar.gz is FTPable from the following
locations and their mirrors:

		ftp.yggdrasil.com:pub/dist/booting
		sunsite.unc.edu:pub/Linux/system/Linux-boot
		tsx-11.mit.edu:pub/linux/sources/sbin

	Please note that once you've made the appropriate
boot floppy, you still still have to set up bootpd, tftpd and
nfsd on your server machine.  Please see the documentation in
the etherboot software package (mentioned below) for some information
on configuring the server machine(s) to support diskless booting.
After you've done that, you still will have to configure the NFS
areas for the root partitions of the diskless clients.


MOTIVATION

	Previously the etherboot-1.1 software package could only
produce a .COM file which could be put a DOS floppy to produce a
network boot floppy, but that floppy disk would contain some
Microsoft copyrighted DOS code, and the correct floppy image would
vary depending on the type of floppy disk media.  As a result,
network boot floppy images were not available simply to download
by FTP.  Anyone who wanted to make a network boot floppy had to
learn quite a bit about the etherboot software package before
being able to create such a disk.  Comboot changes that.
These floppy images are completely freely redistributable
and should work on all types of floppy media.

	This makes it much easier to deploy diskless (or otherwise
network booted) Linux workstations in institutional settings where X
terminals or other types of diskless workstations might be used.

	To make one of these boot diskettes, you just unpack the
tar file and dd the appropriate file onto a floppy disk, like so:

	    tar xfpvz net-boot-floppies-1.1.tar.gz
	    cd net-boot-floppies-1.1
	    dd if=ne.floppy of=/dev/fd0   # Produces an ne2000 boot floppy

	In order to boot from these images, you must have one of the
four types ethernet cards that etherboot-1.1 supports:

		o 3Com 3c503 (3c503.floppy)
		o 3Com 3c509 (3c509.floppy)
		o Western Digital WD80x3 (wd.floppy)
		o Novell NE2000 (ne.floppy)

	The ethernet card on the diskless machine must be located
at IO port 0x300.  On the 3c503, the memory window must also be
enabled.  At the time of this posting, the 3c509 boot floppy was
not tested, but the others were.


REBUILDING FROM SOURCE

	The README file which is included in this software
package describes how to rebuild these floppy images from source.
The source code for these floppy disks consists of the etherboot-1.1
package and the comboot-1.1 boot sector, which you can find in
the following locations:

  etherboot:
    sunsite.unc.edu:/pub/Linux/system/Linux-boot/etherboot-1.1.tar.gz
    ftp.yggdrasil.com:/mirrors/sunsite/system/Linux-Boot/etherboot-1.1.tar.gz
  comboot:
    ftp.yggdrasil.com:/pub/dist/booting/comboot-1.0.tar.gz
    sunsite.unc.edu:/pub/Linux/system/Linux-boot/comboot-1.0.tar.gz
    tsx-11.mit.edu:/pub/linux/source/sbin/comboot-1.0.tar.gz


