ChemApp (C), A programmable thermodynamic calculation interface (COMMERCIAL)

                     *** Now available for Linux! ***

       Keywords: thermodynamics, thermochemistry, process simulation,
                 chemical engineering, free energy minimizer


ChemApp (C) - A New Member of the ChemSage Family

In ChemApp, you now have the powerful calculation capabilities of
ChemSage [1] in the form of a flexible programmable routine. It can be
used to provide powerful application-specific or general tools for
design, testing and production. Like ChemSage, ChemApp is a direct
decendant of SOLGASMIX, developed by the same author.

ChemApp consists of an interface containing a library of FORTRAN
subroutines that link to the data-handling and phase equilibrium
calculation modules of ChemSage. It opens up new horizons for the use
of thermochemical calculations across a wide spectrum of
applications. Furthermore, with GTT's access to appropriate algorithms
and kinetic data, it is also possible to apply the routine to develop
programs for time-dependent (dynamic) applications.

It can be used to design application-specific programs as analytical
tools, for process simulation, or process control; or to integrate
into your own or third party CFD or general simulation programs; or,
to design into teaching tools.

A few lines of easy-programmable code can convert ChemApp into a
powerful computational tool that will enhance your existing
capabilities, or provide totally new ones.


What's it good for?

Its use in developing programs for handling repetitive complex
equilibrium calculations is limitless and can cover an extremely
diverse range of fields of application. Four distinct groups of
applications are possible:

- - The development of application-specific programs for handling
  calculations in well-defined technological areas, as an analytical
  tool to provide a better understanding of processes, and in on-line
  programs for process control.

- - The development of application-specific programs for calculating
  multi-component, chemical reaction mixtures as a series of
  sequential, time-dependent thermochemical states.  Entering a
  pre-defined reactor geometry and known heat and mass-transfer rates
  permits a program to be a practical tool in reactor design.

- - Linking into any third-party process simulation package for
  modelling new or optimising existing processes; for example,
  commercial CFD programs such as Phoenics (tm), Fluent (tm), etc.,
  general simulation programs, including Aspen Plus (tm), Apros (tm),
  etc., and, of course, your own process simulation program.

- - For designing teaching software.


Benefits

ChemApp uses the 'engine' and the data-handling capabilities of the
ChemSage thermochemical application program. This common basis means
that ChemApp reads ordinary ChemSage thermochemical data-files, and
includes the same comprehensive library of excess Gibbs energy models
for various types of solution phases.

In any application, ChemApp is used as an independent module. The
programmable interface provides a link between the rest of the program
and the phase equilibrium module used for calculations, and enables
information to be transferred back-and-forth between the two
parts. This modularisation allows for easy integration of ChemApp into
a program, as well as facilitates the incorporation of future
improvements and extensions to the Routine.


Using the ChemApp Routine

ChemApp is easy to program. Essentially only three programming steps
are necessary to proceed from initialisation of the Routine to
collection of results:

1. initialise the interface, read a thermodynamic data-file, and
   adjust the chemical system,

2. set initial conditions for the equilibrium calculation,

3. perform the calculation and collect results.


In the simplest cases, each programming step is accomplished by
calling one or two subroutines.

For more complicated applications, the number of subroutine calls in
any particular step increase; however, the programming requirements
never become particularly difficult. Error messages are reported on
screen, or stored in a file.


Thermochemical Data for use with ChemApp

GTT-TECHNOLOGIES provides a comprehensive selection of thermochemical
data for use with ChemApp. These data can be in the form of readily
available 'standard' data-files, or 'customised' to meet your specific
requirements.  Data-files are in the form of a text-file and can be
easily updated and refined, if necessary.

ChemApp was developed by Dr. Gunnar Eriksson, based in part on work
done in the framework of a European Science project when at the
Lehrstuhl fuer Theoretische Huettenkunde at the RWTH Aachen, Germany
[2],[3]


References:

[1] ChemSage - A Computer Program for the Calculation of Complex
Chemical Equilibria, G.Eriksson and K.Hack, Metall. Trans. B, 21B
(1990) 1013-1023

[2] A Proposal for a General Thermodynamic Software Interface,
G.Eriksson, H.Sippola, B.Sundman, Proceedings of the Colloquium on
Process Simulation; Helsinki University of Technology, Report
TKK-V-B99 (1994) 67-103

[3] A General Thermodynamic Software Interface, G.Eriksson, P.Spencer,
H.Sippola, Proceedings 2nd Colloquium on Process Simulation, Helsinki
University of Technology, (1995) 113-126


More info on ChemApp, application and code examples can be found on
"Stephan's Technical Thermochemistry Web Page":

http://gttserv.lth.rwth-aachen.de/~sp/tt/

ChemApp is also available for many other platforms.


