- --- CHANGES -------------------------------------------------------------

Dial-Up Utils 1.1:

  - Renamed from 'BBS-Util' because it is not specifically oriented
    towards BBS administration ; it can be used wherever there is a
    modem pool.

  - Support for session time limits.  For example, you can give a user
    90 minutes per day, but only allow 30 minutes per login session.

  - Support for multiple simultaneous guest logins (i.e. each guest
    will be booted at the proper time).

  - Support for "Smart-Boot[tm]" (only booting users when there are no
    free dial-up lines).  To maximize line utilization, you may also
    boot at most one user per minute.  The rationale here is that
    there is no point booting multiple users when only one user is
    waiting to get on.

  - Support for warning users of imminent loss of carrier.

  - Support for longer accounting periods (i.e. months instead of days).

  - New programs 'clean_proc' and 'clean_tmp' help keep /proc and /tmp
    clean ;-)

  - New program 'user_login' simplifies login-time check to ensure a
    user is permitted to use the system.

  - Proper locking is done on the dialup-users file now.

  - I actually read some docs for 'make', so the Makefile doesn't suck
    anymore.

  - Changed identifiers to my new scheme -- i.e.
    user_file_open -> userFileOpen.  What?  You don't care?

BBS-Util 1.0: 

  - New programs 'user_addrec' and 'user_delrec' allow more flexible
    administration.  For example, shadow passwords can be supported
    through this approach.  See the README for details.

  - Assorted minor cleanups.

BBS-Util 0.9: first release


