Greetings,

IDEA (Integrated Document Engineering Assistant - I came up with the
acronym and then invented the full title ;) is a Tcl/Tk interface to a
suite of popular SGML utilities, and at the moment produces DVI, PS and
text output from a single SGML source. See the extract from the
documentation below for more information.

IDEA is currently at version 0.61, and can be considered rather ALPHA.
It does what it does and is fairly stable while doing it, but more
features are being added.

IDEA Home Page with more information and the latest version can be found
at

	http://www.uta.fi/~f1sale/idea/index.shtml

IDEA is GNU GPL'd. 

>From the User's Guide:

IDEA is an SGML document engineering utility for the X Window system.
It is written in Tcl/Tk, and features a (hopefully) user-friendly
graphical user interface for manipulating SGML documents.  The idea
behind IDEA is very simple. It does not do anything remarkable by
itself -- it merely acts as a kind of a launch pad for several SGML
utilities, covering the whole process -- writing, validating,
formatting, previewing and printing the finished document. IDEA also
makes use of the bibliography database features of GNU refer, which
makes referencing other works extremely easy.  The current version of
IDEA supports DeVice Independent (.dvi), PS and ASCII output.  In
addition, IDEA can produce the groff source files for you, in case you
want to edit the source and do the formatting manually.  IDEA owes its
existence mainly to three brilliant SGML tools: sgmls by James Clark,
the QWERTZ DTD and the format toolkit by Tom Gordon, and the PSGML
major mode for Emacs by Lennart Staflin. I started the project to
teach myself Tcl and to speed up my own writing process. I am not
making any claims to being a Tcl programmer - the code is rather
messy, but heck, it works (most of the time.)

Any feedback is greatly appreciated.

Regards,

Sami Lempinen
f1sale@uta.fi


