I have just uploaded yard-1.2, a new release of yard.  This one has
gone through beta testing so it should be reasonably non-bug-infested.

Currently:  file://sunsite.unc.edu/pub/Linux/Incoming/yard-1.2.tar.gz 
Ultimately: file://sunsite.unc.edu/pub/Linux/System/Recovery/yard-1.2.tar.gz

Yard is a suite of Perl scripts for creating and checking rescue disks
(also called emergency floppies, recovery disks, etc).  See OVERVIEW
and FEATURES, below, for a general description.


WHAT'S NEW (v. 1.2)

- - Some bug fixes.  Yard now handles hard-linked files correctly.
  Code cleaned up considerably from 1.0.
   
- - New features:

	- Many more checks on file specifications, configuration
          options and the selected file set.  Also on return codes
	  from system commands.

	- Yard now uses LILO to handle the boot process, so LILO's
          APPEND option is available for non-standard hardware.

	- You can use any size floppies that LILO can handle.  You can
	  put rescue disks on high-capacity (> 1440K) floppies.
	  
	- Verbosity flag allows you to see only important messages,
	  while all messages are saved to a log file.

	- A new double-disk option can put the compressed
	  filesystem on a second disk.  With 66% compression, a second
	  1722K floppy can hold about 5 meg of utilities.  You can
	  play tetris and read news while trying to salvage your hard disk.


OVERVIEW

   Yard is a suite of Perl scripts for creating a so-called boot/root
   rescue disk (a bootable Linux system on one or more floppy disks).
   Rescue disks are used when you can't (or don't want to) boot off
   your hard disk.  They're used mostly in emergencies, and usually
   contain utilities for diagnosing and manipulating hard disks and
   filesystems.

   Most Linux distributions (eg, Slackware and RedHat) come with some
   kind of pre-fabricated rescue disk.  These disks contain a fixed
   set of utilities that are useful but may not be enough in an
   emergency.  For example, if you want to restore files off of backup
   tapes you'll need a restore program that matches your backup
   program.  It's desirable to have a complete, self-contained set of
   up-to-date utilities and kernel.

   Yard is also useful for creating self-contained "Linux-on-a-floppy"
   disks.



FEATURES

- - Yard has a configurable file set so you can specify the files you
  want.  You can use portions of your existing disk structure, and you
  can specify links, file substitutions, wildcards, etc.

- - Yard does numerous checks on the file set to ensure that the rescue
  disk will be usable.  This is the main reason I wrote the package.
  Yard's diagnosis saves time and is more informative than 
  "init: segmentation fault".  Specifically, Yard:

	- Checks symbolic link chains and adds link files if they don't
	  already exist.  Performs a final check for unresolved links.

	- Checks for shared library dependencies of the chosen binaries.
	  Adds the libraries and necessary loader(s).  Performs a
	  final ldconfig to rebuild the ld.so.cache.
	
	- Checks common system files (eg inittab, fstab, rc) for
	  simple mistakes.  Checks that necessary executables exist on
	  rescue disk.  Checks passwd and warns of missing shells and
	  home directories.

	- Checks scripts to make sure interpreters are included.


- - Yard exploits the compressed ramdisk feature of 1.3.48+ kernels.
  With this you can put a kernel image plus a fairly complete root
  filesystem on a single disk.  I can put a 450K compressed zImage
  plus 3.4 megs of utilities (including libs) onto a 1722K disk.  With
  66% compression, the double-disk option will allow about a 5 meg
  root filesystem.


