-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----


Hi everybody.

I have written a program, that I think is worth looking at. It is an
administrative tool, called hifs, Handy Information For Sysadmins.

It's features are:

o - Hifs shows the processes that use the most system resources. System 
resources are can be chosen by the user to be one of: percentage CPU, 
resident set size or total size.
o - Hifs uses only a 26x24 window, which is especially usefull under X, 
using xhifs.
o - Hifs uses very little resources itself.
o - The user kan write messages to or kill processes, if he/she has enough 
priviliges, offcourse.
o - Furthermore, Hifs shows: Total CPU states, free/used memory/swap, load 
averages of the last 1, 5 and 15 minutes and the Number or logins: 
real/total, X/None X.
o - Hifs monitors all the mounted filesystems, and gives a sign when they 
are full. Full is defined by a free space < 1 Mb. This can be changed, but is 
a compile option.
o  - Finally, hifs let's you define two groups of users, you want to monitor. 
This is a compile option, see the readme for more info. These two groups 
consist of a maximum of 8 users. Hifs monitors if these users are: logged in, 
just logged in or not logged in, and it shows this to the user.

The goal of the author was to give as much relevant information about your
system as possible, in a small window, using as little resources as possible.
I think I implemented this goal rather well, and I'd appreciate it if you'd
try it out.

Hifs is available at http://www.stack.urc.tue.nl/~geertj/unix.html, and
it may reside on sunsite (I just uploaded it, so I don't know actually).

Regards,
Geert Jansen (geertj@stack.urc.tue.nl)

