Begin3
Title:          Linux Bootp Client
Version:        V0.50
Entered-date:   1996-Apr-16
Description:	This is a boot protocol client used to grab the machines
		ip number, set up DNS nameservers and other useful information.
Keywords:       bootp bootpc net util
Author:         ceh@eng.cam.ac.uk (Charles Hawkins)
Maintained-by:  J.S.Peatfield@damtp.cam.ac.uk (Jon Peatfield)
Primary-site:   ftp.damtp.cam.ac.uk:/pub/linux/bootpc/bootpc.v050.tgz
Alternate-site: sunsite.unc.edu:/pub/Linux/system/Network/admin/bootpc.v050.tgz
Platform:       You need a BOOTP server too.
Copying-policy: This code is provided as-is, with no warrenty, share and enjoy.
End

Changes since the last release (v0.45) last August include:

  Now compiles cleanly on Alpha (anyone got any other systems to test?)

  Fix of some bugs in TAG handling, and more TAGs now supported
    (including pringing T???= for unregistered TAGs).

  Removal of support for NET-2, as noone has that anymore (I hope) and
    the test I was using to distinguish them went away in 1.3.65 or so.

  New command line option --returniffails to tell it to return if the
  bootpc fails rather than looping, and --waitformore to tell it to
  wait for other bootp replies --- this is very handy to seeing what
  else out there is replying to a client.

  Removal of bootp/DHCP rfcs from distribution, you all know where to
  get them from if you want to read them!

Things missing from this version:

  Still doesn't do DHCP, though many of the dhcp TAGs are supported.

  No manpage (yet).

  Not heavily tested except on a handfull of machines.

The next release will no longer have the --ping option to test for a
machine having the same IP address allocated.  The test didn't work in
many cases, and noone should have such a broken bootp server anyway.
(The code to use this is off by default in rc.bootp now).

Looking at the code in bootptest which comes with bootp.2.4.3 (which
is the bootp server to get in case you didn't know), much of the same
stuff should work for bootpc.  i.e. I may end up submitting a modified
bootpc to be part of that package.

As always I look forward to a flood of but reports.

  -- Jon Peatfield

