For Immediate Release 1996-04-04

Scanning Software for ScanJets under Linux

tummy.com, ltd. has announced XVScan Version 1.22, which adds 
scanner-level gamma correction to the interactive X11 scanning 
and image manipulation program XVScan.

XVScan works under Linux with HP ScanJet series scanners and a supported
SCSI card. XVscan is based on xv, image manipulation software for X
Windows.  It handles 24-bit color, 8-bit greyscale and 1-bit monochrome
images.

XVScan supports many image formats including GIF, JPEG, TIFF (compressed
and uncompressed), PostScript (requires ghostscript),  PBM/PGM/PPM (raw
and ascii), X11 Bitmap, XPM (X PixMap), Sun Rasterfile, BMP, IRIS
Rasterfile, Targa (24-bit), and  FITS (greyscale).

Scanner-level gamma correction will improve color scans by allocating
colors to capture a greater range of detail.

Also included in this release are numerous updates to the ScanJet API
which allows other applications to control an HP ScanJet scanner.

XVScan is also available for HP-UX 9.x, 10.x and FreeBSD.

The list price for XVScan is $50 for ftp or email delivery. It is also 
available on 3.5" floppies or 4mm DAT tape for an additional $15 in the 
United States or $25 internationally.

