Meeting Topic: "Linux and Commercial UNIX: Adversaries or Bedfellows?"

Presented by: Jon 'maddog' Hall [maddog@zk3.dec.com], USG Product
		Management
		
A discussion of Linux vs Commercial UNIX, what each brings to the table
and whether or not they can co-exist in a changing marketplace from a
hardware vendor's perspective.  A discussion of what Digital is doing to
help the Linux market.  Feedback will be requested from the membership
about additional things Digital could do to improve the Linux environment. 

Jon "maddog" Hall is a Senior Manager in Digital's UNIX Software Group. 
Jon has been in the UNIX group for twelve years as an engineer, Product
Manager and Marketing Manager.  Prior to Digital, Jon was a Senior Systems
Administrator in Bell Laboratories' UNIX group, so he has been programming
and using UNIX for over 15 years. 

Recently maddog has been involved with the effort of porting Linux to the
Alpha, which entails having obtained an Alpha system for Linus Torvalds,
funding several speaking trips for Mr. Torvalds (including last year's BCS
meeting at MIT), funding the tsx-11.mit.edu upgrade to an Alpha, and working
directly with the Digital Equipment Corporation engineering group working on
the Linux project.

He is also active in the NNEUUG: The Northern New England Unix User 
Group, which meets monthly in Vermont, New Hampshire & Maine
[http://coos.dartmouth.edu/nneuug/]. And, if that isn't enough, Maddog is 
involved in the Greater New Hamphire Linux User's Group (GNHLUG) 
[http://virgin.mv.com/linux/] Their mailing list address is: 
[gnhlug@zk3.dec.com].

Who:    Boston Computer Society Linux/Unix User's Group
Date:   17 April 1996, Wednesday
Time:   7:00 pm.
Place:  MIT, Building 4, Room 163 
             ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
	
Linux is a UNIX-like operating system built around POSIX standards.  From
its inception less than five years ago, it was developed over the
Internet by a group of people who (for the most part) have never seen
each other, and now runs on an (estimated) 1,000,000 computer systems. 
The operating system (and the source code for it) is free to anyone who
wants it.  This is probably the largest development project ever
accomplished using the Internet. 

